<summary>tests/native-keyboard/tests/chart_on_raised_surface.rs</summary>

```rust
//! Repro: Chart paints its root with `surface` (and a 1px `border`), so on a raised layer
//! (Dialog, Sheet and Popover are all `surface_raised`; an app's dock may be too) every chart
//! is a darker framed block, the same thing #129 was for Table's header. Harness copied from
//! tab_bar_component.rs.

use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::rc::Rc;
use std::time::Duration;

use gpui::{Context, IntoElement, Render, TestAppContext, VisualTestContext, Window, rgba};
use gpui_rhai::*;

struct Host {
    host: ScriptViewHost,
    view: ScriptViewHandle,
}

impl Render for Host {
    fn render(&mut self, _: &mut Window, _: &mut Context<Self>) -> impl IntoElement {
        self.host.container(self.view.element().unwrap())
    }
}

fn mount(cx: &mut TestAppContext, source: String) -> (VisualTestContext, ScriptViewHandle) {
    cx.update(gpui_rhai::install);
    let mut modules = BTreeMap::from([(ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap(), source)]);
    for (id, source) in gpui_rhai_registry::BUNDLED_COMPONENT_SOURCES_BY_ID
        .iter()
        .chain(gpui_rhai_registry::BUNDLED_CHART_SOURCES_BY_ID.iter())
    {
        modules.insert(ModuleId::parse(*id).unwrap(), (*source).to_owned());
    }
    let prepared = EmbeddedScriptView::new(
        ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap(),
        EmbeddedScriptSource::new(modules),
        gpui_rhai_registry::DEFAULT_THEME,
    )
    .token_base(gpui_rhai_registry::TOKEN_BASE_SOURCE)
    .motion_preference(MotionPreference::None)
    .asset_sources(
        gpui_rhai_registry::BUNDLED_ASSET_SOURCES
            .iter()
            .map(|(path, source)| {
                (
                    path.trim_end_matches(".svg").to_owned(),
                    AssetData {
                        mime_type: "image/svg+xml".into(),
                        bytes: source.as_bytes().to_vec(),
                    },
                )
            }),
    )
    .prepare()
    .unwrap();
    let saved = Rc::new(RefCell::new(None));
    let save = saved.clone();
    let window = cx.add_window(move |window, cx| {
        let host = ScriptViewHost::new("table", cx).unwrap();
        let view = prepared
            .mount(ScriptViewConfig::new("table"), host.clone(), window, cx)
            .unwrap();
        *save.borrow_mut() = Some(view.clone());
        Host { host, view }
    });
    cx.run_until_parked();
    let view = saved.borrow().clone().unwrap();
    let mut visual = VisualTestContext::from_window(*window, cx);
    settle(&mut visual);
    (visual, view)
}

fn settle(visual: &mut VisualTestContext) {
    for _ in 0..3 {
        visual.run_until_parked();
        visual.executor().advance_clock(Duration::from_millis(32));
        visual.update(|window, cx| window.simulate_next_frame(cx));
        visual.run_until_parked();
    }
}

const SCRIPT: &str = r#"import "charts/chart" as chart;
fn view(ctx){box([
    chart::Chart(#{key:"c",key_dimension:"id",data:[#{id:"a",x:0,y:0},#{id:"b",x:1,y:1}],
        spec:#{title:"Control",series:[#{key:"s",kind:"line",encode:#{x:"x",y:"y"}}]}})
        .with_style(style().width(px(300)).height(px(160)))
]).with_style(style().width(px(320)).height(px(200)).background(theme_color("surface_raised")))}"#;

/// Painted quads filled with `color`, as (width, height) in scaled pixels.
fn filled(visual: &mut VisualTestContext, color: u32) -> Vec<(f32, f32)> {
    let fill: gpui::Background = rgba(color).into();
    visual.update(|window, _| {
        window
            .painted_quads()
            .iter()
            .filter(|quad| quad.background == fill)
            .map(|quad| (quad.bounds.size.width.0, quad.bounds.size.height.0))
            .collect()
    })
}

#[gpui::test]
fn a_chart_on_a_raised_layer_has_no_block_of_another_surface(cx: &mut TestAppContext) {
    let (mut visual, _view) = mount(cx, SCRIPT.to_owned());
    // default_dark: surface 0x151412, surface_raised 0x1d1c19.
    let raised = filled(&mut visual, 0x1d1c_19ff);
    assert!(!raised.is_empty(), "the raised layer was painted");
    let surface = filled(&mut visual, 0x1514_12ff);
    assert!(
        surface.is_empty(),
        "nothing on the raised layer should be painted with `surface`, got {surface:?}"
    );
}
```

</details>

## Summary

A selected tab draws side hairlines on both edges (`tab_style` in `registry/components/tab_bar.rhai`: every tab reserves `border_left(px(1)).border_right(px(1))`, colored `border` when selected). When the selected tab is the first one, its start hairline sits directly beside the edge line of whatever holds the bar, so that edge reads 2px thick over the bar's height.

A TabBar normally sits against a panel or sidebar edge, so the first tab is the one whose start hairline always has such a neighbour. Found in oh-my-byted: the sidebar's separator runs down past the panel tabs. Measured on a 2x screenshot, the separator is 1pt in the grid area and 2pt next to the bar while the first tab is selected.

## How to reproduce

1. Check out `feat/design-system-0.2` (1179ac2).
2. Save the test below as `tests/native-keyboard/tests/tab_bar_start_edge_hairline.rs`.
3. Run:
   ```
   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test tab_bar_start_edge_hairline
   ```

The reporter's local copy is at `~/.cache/gpui-rhai-1179ac2/tests/native-keyboard/tests/tab_bar_start_edge_hairline.rs`.

Result on 1179ac2: `a_selected_first_tab_does_not_double_the_edge_line` fails with `got start edges at x = [0.0, 1.0]` (the holder's edge line, then the first tab's start hairline 1pt later). The control `control_a_selected_middle_tab_leaves_one_edge_line` passes: with the middle tab selected only the holder's edge is there.

## Suggested fix (for the maintainers to decide)

Let the first tab neither draw nor reserve the start hairline: the holder's edge stands in for it. This is how Zed's tabs treat the first position. Reserving nothing on the first tab, whether or not it is selected, keeps its text from moving when the selection changes. I tried the three-line version (`tab_style(selected, disabled, slotted, index == 0)`, with `border_left(if first { px(0) } else { px(1) })`). The test above passes, and all seven tests in `tab_bar_component.rs` still pass. I did not check RTL or a bar with a `start` slot, where the first tab is not at the edge.

<details>
<summary>tests/native-keyboard/tests/tab_bar_start_edge_hairline.rs</summary>

```rust
//! Repro: a selected tab at the start of the strip draws its start hairline right beside
//! the edge line of whatever holds the bar, so the edge reads 2px thick over the bar's height.
//! A TabBar normally sits against a panel or sidebar edge (in oh-my-byted the sidebar's
//! separator runs down past the bar), and the first tab's start hairline is the only one that
//! always has such a neighbour. Harness copied from tab_bar_component.rs.

use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::rc::Rc;
use std::time::Duration;

use gpui::{Context, IntoElement, Render, TestAppContext, VisualTestContext, Window, rgba};
use gpui_rhai::*;

struct Host {
    host: ScriptViewHost,
    view: ScriptViewHandle,
}

impl Render for Host {
    fn render(&mut self, _: &mut Window, _: &mut Context<Self>) -> impl IntoElement {
        self.host.container(self.view.element().unwrap())
    }
}

fn mount(cx: &mut TestAppContext, source: String) -> (VisualTestContext, ScriptViewHandle) {
    cx.update(gpui_rhai::install);
    let mut modules = BTreeMap::from([(ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap(), source)]);
    for (id, source) in gpui_rhai_registry::BUNDLED_COMPONENT_SOURCES_BY_ID {
        modules.insert(ModuleId::parse(*id).unwrap(), (*source).to_owned());
    }
    let prepared = EmbeddedScriptView::new(
        ModuleId::parse("main").unwrap(),
        EmbeddedScriptSource::new(modules),
        gpui_rhai_registry::DEFAULT_THEME,
    )
    .token_base(gpui_rhai_registry::TOKEN_BASE_SOURCE)
    .motion_preference(MotionPreference::None)
    .asset_sources(
        gpui_rhai_registry::BUNDLED_ASSET_SOURCES
            .iter()
            .map(|(path, source)| {
                (
                    path.trim_end_matches(".svg").to_owned(),
                    AssetData {
                        mime_type: "image/svg+xml".into(),
                        bytes: source.as_bytes().to_vec(),
                    },
                )
            }),
    )
    .prepare()
    .unwrap();
    let saved = Rc::new(RefCell::new(None));
    let save = saved.clone();
    let window = cx.add_window(move |window, cx| {
        let host = ScriptViewHost::new("tabs", cx).unwrap();
        let view = prepared
            .mount(ScriptViewConfig::new("tabs"), host.clone(), window, cx)
            .unwrap();
        *save.borrow_mut() = Some(view.clone());
        Host { host, view }
    });
    cx.run_until_parked();
    let view = saved.borrow().clone().unwrap();
    let mut visual = VisualTestContext::from_window(*window, cx);
    settle(&mut visual);
    (visual, view)
}

fn settle(visual: &mut VisualTestContext) {
    for _ in 0..3 {
        visual.run_until_parked();
        visual.executor().advance_clock(Duration::from_millis(32));
        visual.update(|window, cx| window.simulate_next_frame(cx));
        visual.run_until_parked();
    }
}

/// The bar inside a box whose start edge is a 1px `border` line, as against a sidebar edge.
fn script(selected: &str) -> String {
    format!(
        r#"import "components/tab_bar" as tab_bar;
fn tabs(){{[#{{value:"t0",label:"First"}},#{{value:"t1",label:"Second"}},#{{value:"t2",label:"Third"}}]}}
fn changed(ctx,value){{}}
fn view(ctx){{box([
    tab_bar::TabBar(#{{key:"files",label:"Open files",value:"{selected}",tabs:tabs(),on_change:Fn("changed")}})
]).with_style(style().width(px(400)).border_left(px(1)).border_color(theme_color("border")))}}"#
    )
}

/// Distinct x positions (points) of painted `border`-colored start edges within 2pt of the
/// window's left edge. The scene can hold a quad more than once, so positions are deduplicated.
fn start_edges(visual: &mut VisualTestContext) -> Vec<f32> {
    // default_dark `border`.
    let line: gpui::Hsla = rgba(0x3a38_33ff).into();
    visual.update(|window, _| {
        let scale = window.scale_factor();
        window
            .painted_quads()
            .iter()
            .filter(|quad| {
                quad.border_widths.left.0 > 0.0
                    && quad.border_color == line
                    && quad.bounds.origin.x.0 / scale < 2.0
            })
            .map(|quad| quad.bounds.origin.x.0 / scale)
            .fold(Vec::new(), |mut xs, x| {
                if !xs.iter().any(|seen: &f32| (seen - x).abs() < 0.25) {
                    xs.push(x);
                }
                xs
            })
    })
}

#[gpui::test]
fn a_selected_first_tab_does_not_double_the_edge_line(cx: &mut TestAppContext) {
    let (mut visual, _view) = mount(cx, script("t0"));
    let edges = start_edges(&mut visual);
    assert_eq!(
        edges.len(),
        1,
        "only the holder's edge line should be at the start, got start edges at x = {edges:?}"
    );
}

#[gpui::test]
fn control_a_selected_middle_tab_leaves_one_edge_line(cx: &mut TestAppContext) {
    let (mut visual, _view) = mount(cx, script("t1"));
    assert_eq!(start_edges(&mut visual).len(), 1);
}
```

</details>

## Summary

Table paints its header row with `surface` (`registry/components/table.rhai`, the `header` row: `.border_bottom(px(1)).border_color(theme_color("border")).background(theme_color("surface"))`), while the body rows are transparent and take the color of whatever they sit on. On a raised layer the header therefore becomes a band of a different color across the table. The raised layers are the registry's own: Dialog, Sheet and Popover are all `surface_raised`.

Found in oh-my-byted: its tool dock (a side layer like Sheet) is now `surface_raised`, and the tools in it are mostly Tables, so every docked table shows a darker header strip. The `header` part could be overridden by the caller, but a widget does not know which layer it is mounted on, so it cannot pick the right color.

How visible it is depends on the theme: default_dark `0x151412` vs `0x1d1c19`; gruvbox `0x282828` vs `0x3c3836`; catppuccin mocha `0x1e1e2e` vs `0x313244`.

## How to reproduce

1. Check out `feat/design-system-0.2` (1179ac2).
2. Save the test below as `tests/native-keyboard/tests/table_header_on_raised_surface.rs`.
3. Run:
   ```
   cargo test --manifest-path tests/native-keyboard/Cargo.toml --test table_header_on_raised_surface
   ```

The reporter's local copy is at `~/.cache/gpui-rhai-1179ac2/tests/native-keyboard/tests/table_header_on_raised_surface.rs`.

Result on 1179ac2: fails with ``nothing on the raised layer should be painted with `surface`, got [(640.0, 64.0)]``: one quad, the table's width by the header row (scaled pixels), filled with `surface` on a `surface_raised` box. The control assertion (the raised layer itself was painted) passes.

## Suggested fix (for the maintainers to decide)

Leave the header unfilled, as the rows are, so it takes the layer's color. With only the `.background(theme_color("surface"))` call removed, the test above passes; I did not check whether the fill is there for another reason (for example a header that stays over rows scrolled under it). If it is needed, a fill that follows the layer (a token or environment value a raised layer sets) would keep it.

