### Seen in a real app

oh-my-byted mounts several adorned cluster tables in one view. It first renders cached rows and then swaps in freshly fetched rows, with no scrolling involved. On 0.1.8 this failed in 2 of 3 runs with the same error. On 0.1.7 it passed all 3 runs with the same data and cache.

## Analysis

- `render_table_row` (`registry/components/table.rhai`) creates each adornment badge without a `key` prop. It calls `.with_key(...)` on the returned node, which does not take part in the component instance path:
  ```rhai
  content.push(badge::Badge(#{ text: adornment.text, variant: adornment.variant,
      dot: adornment.dot, size: "sm" }).with_key(`${cell.key}-adornment-${index}`));
  ```
- A keyless formal component gets the automatic key `callsite#occurrence`, with the occurrence counted in `active.positions` per parent and call site (`engine.rs` `reserve_component_path`, around L3050). All rows share the parent `VirtualCollection[tce-table-body]`, so their badges are told apart only by that counter.
- `retain_virtual_component_manifest` (`engine.rs`, around L2785) adds the retained rows' paths to `active.seen` but does not advance `active.positions`. The first newly realized row therefore numbers its badge `#0` again, which equals a retained row's path, and `reserve_component_path` rejects it.

From this reading, any keyless formal component inside a `virtual_collection` item renderer can hit the same collision. We have not tested renderers other than Table.

## Suggestion (for the maintainers to decide)

This is only what we tried locally. Please choose whatever fix fits the runtime design.

- **Registry-level.** The single change that made the test above and the oh-my-byted scenario pass was a row-scoped `key` prop on the badge. With it, that app's full smoke run also passed: 17 panels and 14 resident views, 0 failures.
  ```rhai
  content.push(badge::Badge(#{ key: `${payload.key}-${cell.key}-adornment-${index}`,
      text: adornment.text, variant: adornment.variant,
      dot: adornment.dot, size: "sm" }).with_key(`${cell.key}-adornment-${index}`));
  ```
- **Runtime-level ideas** (not tried). Automatic `callsite#occurrence` keys may not be a stable identity inside virtual items, since the same row can receive a different occurrence number in different batches. Possible directions:
  - give each item render its own path segment, for example `VirtualCollection[body]/Item[<row key>]/…`;
  - or report keyless formal components inside item renderers with an explicit diagnostic.

## Context

oh-my-byted exposes host capabilities to third-party widgets. Some of them should only run in response to the user, e.g. `omb.open` (switch the user to another tool) — similar to how browsers gate `window.open` / clipboard writes behind user activation.

Today a `CapabilityHandler` only sees `(method, input)`:

```rust
fn call(&mut self, method: &str, input: UiValue) -> Result<UiValue, String>;
```

so the host cannot tell whether a call comes from a pointer/key handler, a `timeout`, a `start_task` completion, a subscription push, or an effect. We tried inferring it by time ("was there any window input in the last 1.5 s?"). That is a heuristic with real problems:

- **False positives**: any click anywhere unlocks every view's timers for the window.
- **No attribution**: it can't tell which view the user interacted with.
- **Coupled to the host's element tree**: our first version listened with a root `capture_any_mouse_down`, which never fires for clicks inside `occlude()`d overlays because element listeners require `hitbox.is_hovered` (`div.rs`). Users saw "clicking a row does nothing, but press ↓ first and then it works". Switching to `window.on_mouse_event` from a zero-size canvas fixes that, but it's still a guess.
- **Tests have to fake input**, because `AutomationCommand::Dispatch` calls handlers directly.

We are dropping the gate on our side for now (rate limit + call log instead). This issue is about giving hosts the precise signal so gating can be done properly later.

## Ask

Pass the invocation origin to capability handlers, e.g.

```rust
pub enum InvocationOrigin {
    UserInput { event: &'static str },   // pointer / key / click dispatched from a platform input event
    Automation,                          // AutomationCommand::Dispatch
    Timer, TaskCompletion, Subscription, Effect, Render,
}

pub trait CapabilityHandler {
    fn call(&mut self, method: &str, input: UiValue) -> Result<UiValue, String>;
    /// Default forwards to `call`, so existing handlers keep compiling.
    fn call_with(&mut self, origin: &InvocationOrigin, method: &str, input: UiValue)
        -> Result<UiValue, String> { self.call(method, input) }
}
```

The runtime already knows which path it is on when it invokes a script callback; synchronous helper calls inside the same dispatch keep the same origin. A handler that starts a task and calls the capability in the task callback gets `TaskCompletion` (that is the point).

**Performance**: this should be an enum set on the dispatch path (a field on the existing invocation / context struct, or a stack slot restored on return) and read only by handlers that ask for it — no allocation, no locking, nothing per frame. If it can't be done without measurable cost on the event / render path, we're fine without it.

Also useful (optional): let the host see which view the event was dispatched to, so activation can be attributed per view.
